<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Silvius</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Silvius is a legendary king of Alba Longa, in most Roman accounts the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aeneas">Aeneas</a> and Lavinia and the successor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ascanius">Ascanius</a>. The name Silvius was said to have been borne by all later Alban kings, a line that ended with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/numitor">Numitor</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amulius">Amulius</a>. These kings appear in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livy">Livy</a>, Dionysius of Halicarnassus and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ovid">Ovid</a> but are generally regarded as invention.</p></div>
